Z||000002||SPDFREK||06-02-2001||11:08 PM||||Yep Yep Yep, my friend Jeff has some like this, Not sure if they are the same. however he did spend like $56 us with shipping and tax. The ones he has work great, and after awhile he bought the "plasma" lights for it and they light up like day. Again I am not sure this is the set, but ya can't beat the price. only complaint he had was the wiring. (had to splice into wires instead of the kit coming with and adapter.)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/test_2.gif"> <P>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||06-02-2001||11:29 PM||||Well I emailed the seller to see if rewiring was necessary.  I've wanted one of these conversion kits for a while now, because with sealed beam headlights everytime you replace a bulb you have to readjust them.  And if these are much brighter too, that'd be sweet.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jefrysuko||06-03-2001||12:11 AM||||From what I understand about these kits you actually have to pull the housing out just to replace the bulb.  Thus it does have to be readjusted everytime you change the bulb.<P>Can anyone confirm this?<P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A>  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Zippy_11.jpg">                    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT_4.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||06-03-2001||01:29 AM||||I have my doubts as to that being an "HID" system.<BR>Firstly, an HID system is a computer-tuned reflecor and lens system coupled with a special type of Xenon buld. You can tell an HID system, 'cos it tends to "shift" colors at different angles. This doesn't. From what I can see it's simply a dyed bulb. <BR>Still, it DOES look like a nifty blue. Too bad it's illegal up here. I've had great experiences with Sylvania's Cool Blue lamps. I like the brilliant white. I'll stick to those. Lot less money  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000006||SPDFREK||06-03-2001||12:29 PM||||ok, I emailed bout the wiring and if ya had to jump up a size with fuses, here is the answer.<P>the base of the bulb is the same as your factory headlamp no modifications<BR>needed<BR>----- Original Message -----<BR>From: &lt;jkolley@tampabay.rr.com&gt;<BR>To: &lt;bulbs@prostreetlighting.com&gt;<BR>Sent: Saturday, June 02, 2001 8:25 PM<BR>Subject: Question for seller -- Item #591895527<P><BR>&gt; I'm just wondering If the lights come with an adapter for the original<BR>light plugs, or if this is a splice the wire kit. Secondly, do you have to<BR>jump up to a new size fuse for the lights, or use the same fuse as original?<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; Thx for the time and info<P>hope that helps.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/test_2.gif"> <P>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||06-03-2001||12:36 PM||||This is not HID. It is made to look like the blue HID lights. But they are still a lot brighter and more intense than stock. Go to their website and read the ad carefully. I still think it is a great deal and I ordered mine for $38. <P>The only thing I wonder about is where to get replacement bulbs when these blow. I am sure you can't just walk into Wal-Mart and buy them. Probably have to order from these guys again.<P>And as far as replacing the bulbs, looks like a halogen type setup to me from the pictures on their site. Just twist the back of the housing and pull out the connector. Real easy. I'll know more when mine get here.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-03-2001||04:45 PM||||HID lamp systems use a high frequency arc lamp. the lamp is similar to those used in Photo strobe lamps. There are conversion systems for certain specific, and very expencive, cars. These kits typically cost several hundred dollars. There are no generic HID systems I'm aware of. The big reason is that HID is not certified for U.S. aftermarket installation. I'm not sure they've even certified it for the luxury class yet.<P>Beyond that... There are a number of types of lamps on the atermarket. Your first question should be... Are they Street Legal? Overly bright headlights, especially if they are not carefully aimed, generally tend to make you a ticket magnet.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||06-03-2001||05:40 PM||||Yes, I am fully aware that these arent true HID setups.  That is the reason HID is in quotes in the topic name.  I know how true HID works, and that they arent cheap.  I was looking in a catalog for my friends car (Bavarian Autosport), and the ture HID conv kit for some late model bimmers were upwards of $1200.<P>But still, if they are brighter, its a good deal compared to the other setups similar to that that I have been shopping around for.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||06-03-2001||07:01 PM||||I have a set of HID lights, that would fit into the Fiero with a little modification. These lights are not as tall as the stock fiero ones, but they are the same width. I am working on doing an install where the lights only pop-up about halfway to expose the lights. Still in progress. The kit ran me $580, and they are sold on what type of socket your car has. I am using a set of lights bayonets from a Ford Van, and got the conversion kit for those bayonets. Should look pretty damn awesome when done.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Songman||06-03-2001||07:15 PM||||I love the lazy headlight look... I forget who it is that has that red GT with them, but it is great...<P>And I was just stating that it was not true HID for some folks that may not have known.  I didn't know till I went to their site...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroJoe||06-03-2001||09:06 PM||||It's a good marketing ploy though, claiming these are HID.  Oh well, if the blue tint makes them look cool and they are bright, it'll be worth it.<P>I've looked some more, and they are still the best deal around.  I'm going to go place my bid right now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ditz88GT||06-04-2001||09:42 AM||||Hey Songman, I have the same question about where to buy the bulbs.  I got a set of them and they installed really easy, but now, if the light goes out...can I buy these bulbs anywhere else?<P>Ditz88GT <A HREF="http://go.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://go.to/fiero</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Ditz88GT (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
